    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n6738" root="جوف" body-key="j">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مُجَوَّفٌ</span></b> : see <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَجْوَفُ</span>, in three places. ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b2-  Also A
beast <i>whose</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بَلَق</span> [q. v.] <i>reaches up to his belly:</i>
(As, S, K:) or a horse <i>whose</i> <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">بَلَق</span> <i>reaches to his
sides</i> is said to be <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مُجَوَّفٌ بَلَقًا</span>. (AA, TA.) [See
also <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَجْوَفُ</span>, last meaning but one.] ― 
       <p class="alpheios-lex-sense-separator"> </p><span class="alpheios-lex-sense-spacer2"> </span>-b3-  And an
epithet applied to the bird called <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">صُرَد</span>, because it
is <i>white in the belly.</i> (Mgh and Msb in art. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">صرد</span>.)

